K-Means vs Hierarchical Comparison (2-3 lines):
------------------------------------------------------------ 

1. K-Means clusters are balanced (25,527 / 28,761 / 16,404 rows); the
   hierarchical cut on the 2,500-row sample is much more skewed
   (1,588 / 850 / 62 rows).
2. Both methods recover the same risk gradient: the cluster with the
   highest diabetes rate also has the highest BMI and high-BP rate.
3. K-Means handled all 70,692 rows in seconds, while hclust needs an
   O(n^2) distance matrix, which is why it ran on a sample only.

